  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nAn upper level low over the Great Lakes will continue to\nprogress eastward while an upper level ridge over the Northern \nRockies continues to amplify. Northeasterly surface flow is \ncurrently propagating low stratus across the central and eastern\nhalf of the state. Low stratus is forecast to break up later \nthis afternoon as northeasterly flow switches to southeasterly\nand solar insolation burns off the westerly extent of the low \nstratus. Although, some low stratus may linger in the James \nRiver Valley to the Red River Valley. Some patchy to areas of \nfog is possible across the northwest as temperatures near the \ndew point with little to no wind. Fog may be dense at times. \nPatchy fog will burn off after sunrise. High temperatures are \nforecast to be around the upper 30s east due to the low clouds \nand the lower 50s out west due to the proximity of the upper \nlevel ridge. Overnight low temperatures are forecast to drop \ndown into the 20s.\n\nThe upper level ridge will pass over the Northern Plain Friday  \nbringing back above normal temperatures in the 50s out west \nwhile the east is forecast to be in the 40s. Temperatures are \nforecast to warm up into the 50s and 60s Saturday as the thermal\nridge moves over the region and compressional warming ahead of \na frontal passage. Upper level ridging will move eastward this \nSaturday as an upper level low digs across the International \nborder. The aforementioned upper level low will bring forth \nwindy conditions across the western and central half of the \nstate as westerly winds aid the warmer temperatures Saturday \nafternoon. No precipitation is forecast from this upper low. \nRidging will build back over western CONUS Sunday, but an upper \nlevel wave will dampen the ridge Monday leading to windy \nconditions with another frontal passage. Upper level ridging \nwill then amplify over western CONUS by the middle of next week \nbringing above normal temperatures and dry conditions.",
